<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The window Android paints while the process starts, before Flutter has
    drawn anything. Removed automatically on the first Flutter frame.

    Two layers: the app's own background colour, and the mark centred on it.

    **The mark here is an empty tray — no cards in it — and that is on
    purpose.** This window is painted by the OS, so nothing on it can move.
    SplashPage draws the identical empty tray at the identical 96dp in the
    identical place, so the handover from the OS to the app is invisible, and
    then files the three cards into it. Anything the launch window had already
    shown is never re-introduced; the only thing that arrives is the thing that
    was never here.

    `splash_logo` is a real raster now (generated per density by
    `tool/generate_brand_assets.dart`), so wrapping it in <bitmap> is both
    legal and the right form — it is what pins the gravity. It used to be a
    vector, and <bitmap> around a vector fails to inflate; because this
    drawable is the activity's starting window, that failure took the whole app
    down before Flutter ever started.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/splash_background" />
    <item>
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/splash_logo"
            android:gravity="center" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
